WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Jaime Ochoa
Reviewer: Elisabeth Buffard

00:00:06.955 --> 00:00:08.711
In my past life as a soccer coach,

00:00:08.711 --> 00:00:10.361
once you won a national championship,

00:00:10.361 --> 00:00:12.109
everyone wants to come play for you.

00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:13.403
Really not true.

00:00:13.403 --> 00:00:17.233
Once you paid them
$ 25,000 a year in scholarships,

00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:18.870
everybody wants 
to come play for you.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.882
And parents would always
come to me and they'd say:

00:00:20.882 --> 00:00:24.298
"Okay, my son or my daughter
wants to come play at your university,

00:00:24.298 --> 00:00:26.085
what is it that we have to do?

00:00:26.085 --> 00:00:27.478
You know, what are you looking for?"

00:00:27.478 --> 00:00:29.396
And being the Socratic
professor that I am,

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:31.370
I say, well, what does
your son or daughter do?

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.778
What do they do really well
that we'd be interested in?

00:00:33.778 --> 00:00:36.863
And typically their answers are,
well, they've got great vision.

00:00:36.863 --> 00:00:37.845
They're really good.

00:00:37.845 --> 00:00:39.005
They can see the entire field.

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:42.620
Or, my daughter is the fastest player,
there's nobody that can beat her.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.079
Or, my son's got a great left-footer.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:47.484
Really great in the air
and can hit every ball.

00:00:47.484 --> 00:00:50.197
I'm like: "Yeah, not bad;

00:00:50.197 --> 00:00:52.250
but to be quite honest with you,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.298
those are the last things
I'm looking for.

00:00:54.298 --> 00:00:55.695
The most important thing?

00:00:57.024 --> 00:00:58.157
Self-confidence."

00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:01.890
Without that skill, and I use
the word skill intentionally,

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:06.267
without that skill, we are
useless as a soccer player.

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.945
Because when you lose sight
or belief in yourself,

00:01:09.945 --> 00:01:11.495
we're done for.

00:01:11.495 --> 00:01:13.486
I use the definition 
of self-confidence

00:01:13.486 --> 00:01:16.024
to be the ability or the belief

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:20.282
to believe in yourself,
to accomplish any task,

00:01:20.282 --> 00:01:23.019
no matter the odds,
no matter the difficulty,

00:01:23.019 --> 00:01:24.241
no matter the adversity.

00:01:24.241 --> 00:01:28.536
The belief that you can
accomplished it, self-confidence.

00:01:28.536 --> 00:01:31.289
Some of you are saying,
"Great, I don't have it. I'm so shy.

00:01:31.289 --> 00:01:33.324
I'll never do that, bla, bla, bla."

00:01:33.324 --> 00:01:37.290
And you start to drag
all the way down here.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.500
But, I use the word skill

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.350
because

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.881
I believe it can be trained.

00:01:41.881 --> 00:01:44.121
And I'll show you a couple
of ways in which we do.

00:01:44.121 --> 00:01:45.554
Hopefully I won't run out of time.

00:01:45.554 --> 00:01:46.437
I don't use any slides

00:01:46.437 --> 00:01:48.958
because my speech always goes
here, or here, or here.

00:01:48.958 --> 00:01:50.802
So we'll see which way we get to.

00:01:50.802 --> 00:01:53.456
The easiest way
to build self-confidence:

00:01:53.456 --> 00:01:54.633
there's no magic button.

00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:56.125
I can't say: "Hey, this plane
is going down,

00:01:56.125 --> 00:01:56.897
who can fly?

00:01:56.897 --> 00:01:57.661
Put your hand up."

00:01:57.661 --> 00:01:59.381
"I can, I'm confident!"

00:01:59.381 --> 00:02:01.926
(Laughter)

00:02:01.926 --> 00:02:05.459
Repetition, repetition, repetition.

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:06.447
Right?

00:02:06.447 --> 00:02:09.573
What does Malcom Gladwell call it,
the 10,000-hour rule?

00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:10.579
There's no magic button.

00:02:10.579 --> 00:02:13.138
I recruited a goalie from Colombia,
South America one year.

00:02:13.138 --> 00:02:15.926
Big, tall 6'3" man.

00:02:15.926 --> 00:02:18.481
You know, he had hands like stone.

00:02:18.481 --> 00:02:19.767
I thought he was like Flipper.

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:22.512
Everytime I threw him the ball,
down, onto the ground.

00:02:22.512 --> 00:02:24.199
I was like, oh my god,
we're in trouble.

00:02:24.199 --> 00:02:26.870
Simple solution: get to the wall,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.494
kick a ball against
the wall and catch it.

00:02:28.494 --> 00:02:30.357
kick the ball against
the wall and catch it.

00:02:30.357 --> 00:02:33.327
His goal was 350 a day
for eight months.

00:02:33.327 --> 00:02:36.162
He came back,
his hands were calloused,

00:02:36.162 --> 00:02:38.785
the moisture on his hands
were literally gone,

00:02:38.785 --> 00:02:40.266
he is now playing in Europe.

00:02:40.266 --> 00:02:41.815
Magic? No.

00:02:41.815 --> 00:02:44.522
Repetition, repetition, repetition.

00:02:44.522 --> 00:02:47.138
The problem is, we expect
to be self-confident

00:02:47.138 --> 00:02:49.616
but we can't be unless the skill,

00:02:49.616 --> 00:02:51.028
or the task we're doing,

00:02:51.028 --> 00:02:53.068
is not novel, is not new to us.

00:02:53.068 --> 00:02:55.354
We want to be in a situation

00:02:55.354 --> 00:02:58.156
where we have so
much pressure in that

00:02:58.156 --> 00:03:00.452
and what I mean, cause
pressure builds diamonds,

00:03:00.452 --> 00:03:02.482
we want to be in a situation where

00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:04.932
"hey, I've done this
a thousand times".

00:03:04.932 --> 00:03:06.038
I did my speech,

00:03:06.038 --> 00:03:08.488
and I practiced in front of a mirror:
bla, bla, bla, bla, bla, bla.

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:09.710
Hey I'm sounding good.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.598
And then I went
in front of my kids, and my wife.

00:03:12.598 --> 00:03:14.265
I said, oh gosh, 
I got a little nervous.

00:03:14.265 --> 00:03:15.392
Then I'd get in front of Glenn Gould,

00:03:15.392 --> 00:03:17.407
Oh my goodness,
I am a little more nervous!

00:03:17.407 --> 00:03:19.346
By the time I get to the ACG,

00:03:19.346 --> 00:03:22.446
where 2,500 people,
can't say anymore, right?

00:03:22.446 --> 00:03:24.398
Twenty-five hundred people,

00:03:24.398 --> 00:03:26.898
where twenty-five hundred
people are there,

00:03:26.898 --> 00:03:29.517
I won't have a single
ounce of nervousness

00:03:29.517 --> 00:03:32.510
because of my ability to practice.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:33.424
Right?

00:03:33.424 --> 00:03:34.992
Over, and over, and over, again.

00:03:34.992 --> 00:03:36.837
The problem with repetition is:

00:03:36.837 --> 00:03:40.337
how many of us bail
after the first bit of failure?

00:03:40.337 --> 00:03:43.252
How many of us bail after
the first bit of adversity?

00:03:43.252 --> 00:03:44.816
Edison was on that video,

00:03:44.816 --> 00:03:46.492
and it depends who you ask,

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:47.907
there's anywhere from 1,000

00:03:47.907 --> 00:03:50.365
to 10,000 tries
to build that light bulb.

00:03:50.365 --> 00:03:52.450
1,000 to 10,000.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.193
J.K Rowling should be on that video.

00:03:54.193 --> 00:03:57.044
Do you know how many publishers
she took her Harry Potter book to?

00:03:57.044 --> 00:03:59.520
I believe the number was 12 or 13...

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.390
I am pretty confident but
after two or three noes

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:03.456
I'd be like: "damn it!".

00:04:03.456 --> 00:04:05.047
After six or seven, 
I'm like: "maybe not!"

00:04:05.047 --> 00:04:07.068
Definitely after nine or ten

00:04:07.068 --> 00:04:08.505
I'd be looking to be a soccer coach

00:04:08.505 --> 00:04:09.819
or something else besides an author.

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:10.736
(Laughter)

00:04:10.736 --> 00:04:11.461
Right?

00:04:11.461 --> 00:04:14.054
I mean, twelve times somebody said no.

00:04:14.054 --> 00:04:18.741
But, practice, practice, practice,
and do not accept failure.

00:04:18.741 --> 00:04:20.110
Maybe it shouldn't be repetition,

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.075
maybe the answer 
should be persistence.

00:04:22.075 --> 00:04:25.961
Because we all repeat something but
very few of us really will persist.

00:04:25.961 --> 00:04:28.905
So that's one way 
to build self-confidence.

00:04:28.905 --> 00:04:30.044
Get out there.

00:04:30.044 --> 00:04:33.013
Do what you want to do
and do not accept no.

00:04:33.013 --> 00:04:34.988
The other one is self-talk.

00:04:34.988 --> 00:04:37.197
We all have a self-talk tape
that plays in our head.

00:04:37.197 --> 00:04:40.181
Anybody go shopping and
put on a pair of pants this week?

00:04:40.181 --> 00:04:41.422
If you're a woman,

00:04:41.422 --> 00:04:42.990
the first thing that always comes:

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.839
"damn I look fat in these pants!."

00:04:44.839 --> 00:04:46.657
And if you're a man,
it's the opposite:

00:04:46.657 --> 00:04:49.077
"Oh god, I got no muscle,
I'm so flabby!"

00:04:49.077 --> 00:04:52.346
Right? We all have this tape
that plays in our head.

00:04:52.346 --> 00:04:54.801
As a student,
if they asked me the question,

00:04:54.801 --> 00:04:58.439
it was like: "Oh, gee please professor
don't pick me, I don't know the answer."

00:04:58.439 --> 00:04:59.670
I'd look down.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:00.331
Right?

00:05:00.331 --> 00:05:02.764
If you're in the b...when I,
let me tell you something,

00:05:02.764 --> 00:05:05.607
and the VP of business adminis.
here, I shouldn't repeat this,

00:05:05.607 --> 00:05:07.531
but when they hired me
as an Athletics director,

00:05:07.531 --> 00:05:09.990
I sat in an architect's meeting,

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.752
and I am as dumb as a post

00:05:11.752 --> 00:05:14.384
when it comes to anything to do
with numbers and angles.

00:05:14.384 --> 00:05:15.209
And they are like:

00:05:15.209 --> 00:05:16.314
the fundibulator valve

00:05:16.314 --> 00:05:18.263
of the architectural, uh,

00:05:18.263 --> 00:05:21.405
what do you think doctor Joseph?

00:05:21.405 --> 00:05:23.959
Uh, let me look into that for you
and get back to you.

00:05:23.959 --> 00:05:25.203
(Laughter)

00:05:25.203 --> 00:05:26.019
Right?

00:05:26.019 --> 00:05:30.352
I was in a, oh god god, please don't
ask me, please don't ask me.

00:05:30.352 --> 00:05:34.036
We all have this negative
self-talk that goes in our head.

00:05:34.036 --> 00:05:35.564
Guess what?

00:05:35.564 --> 00:05:39.241
There's enough people that 
are telling us we can't do it.

00:05:39.241 --> 00:05:40.635
That we're not good enough.

00:05:40.635 --> 00:05:43.433
Why do we want to tell ourselves that?

00:05:43.433 --> 00:05:47.644
We know for a fact that
thoughts influence actions.

00:05:47.644 --> 00:05:51.185
We saw it there with the video
Sheldon, Dr. Levy showed.

00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:53.433
We know that our
thoughts influence actions,

00:05:53.433 --> 00:05:56.578
why do we want to say that
negative self-talk to ourselves?

00:05:56.578 --> 00:05:59.583
We need to get 
our own self-affirmations.

00:05:59.583 --> 00:06:02.323
Muhammad Ali, what was
his self-affirmation?

00:06:02.323 --> 00:06:04.951
I am the greatest!

00:06:06.571 --> 00:06:08.548
Who else is going to tell you?

00:06:08.548 --> 00:06:10.726
There need to be quiet
moments in your bedroom,

00:06:10.726 --> 00:06:12.690
quiet moments when
you're brushing your teeth.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:14.594
That we need to reaffirm:

00:06:14.594 --> 00:06:18.965
"I am the captain of my ship
and the master of my fate!"

00:06:18.965 --> 00:06:21.050
That is my affirmation.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.696
I came from a school
of one thousand people,

00:06:23.696 --> 00:06:26.342
I lived in a town of one thousand
people for fifteen years;

00:06:26.342 --> 00:06:29.762
there's no reason that I should be
in charge of an Athletics department,

00:06:29.762 --> 00:06:31.647
building maple leaf gardens.

00:06:31.647 --> 00:06:35.464
But I am the captain of my ship
and the master of my fate.

00:06:35.464 --> 00:06:38.661
If I don't say it, 
if I don't believe it,

00:06:38.661 --> 00:06:40.537
no one else will.

00:06:40.537 --> 00:06:43.161
How do you build self-confidence?

00:06:43.161 --> 00:06:46.139
Get away from the people
who will tear you down.

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:47.497
There's enough of that.

00:06:47.497 --> 00:06:53.024
Muhammad Ali, I am the greatest!
There is no one better than me.

00:06:53.024 --> 00:06:57.075
There's a difference between
hubris, and ego, and false pride.

00:06:57.075 --> 00:07:00.372
It's just reminding yourself
in quiet silent moments,

00:07:00.372 --> 00:07:02.484
I put it down on a list,

00:07:02.484 --> 00:07:04.126
it's right beside my mirror,

00:07:04.126 --> 00:07:05.088
right?

00:07:05.088 --> 00:07:07.132
about all the things that
make me who I am.

00:07:07.132 --> 00:07:09.133
Because I make enough mistakes,

00:07:09.133 --> 00:07:11.354
and the newspapers will recognize it,

00:07:11.354 --> 00:07:13.222
and people around me
will recognize it;

00:07:13.222 --> 00:07:14.842
and they'll tear me down,

00:07:14.842 --> 00:07:17.209
and pretty soon 
I'll begin to believe it.

00:07:17.209 --> 00:07:20.578
There was a time when
my confidence was really low.

00:07:20.578 --> 00:07:22.463
There was a time when I took this job

00:07:22.463 --> 00:07:24.293
when I came from Iowa,

00:07:24.293 --> 00:07:27.228
I don't know if I could do it.

00:07:27.228 --> 00:07:29.649
I had to bring out
my self-confidence letter.

00:07:29.649 --> 00:07:32.633
A letter I wrote to myself
when I was feeling good.

00:07:32.633 --> 00:07:36.132
Ivan, congratulations on
getting your PhD before 40.

00:07:36.132 --> 00:07:40.584
Congra...I am 40, under.

00:07:40.584 --> 00:07:41.864
(Laughter)

00:07:41.864 --> 00:07:44.040
Congratulations on winning
a national championship.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:48.151
Good job on raising three good kids
and marrying the right woman.

00:07:48.151 --> 00:07:49.938
I wrote a letter to myself,

00:07:49.938 --> 00:07:51.787
it was my own brag sheet.

00:07:51.787 --> 00:07:54.986
My own letter about
the things I was proud of.

00:07:54.986 --> 00:07:56.337
Because there are moments,

00:07:56.337 --> 00:08:00.129
and we'll all experience them
in our career, in our lives,

00:08:00.129 --> 00:08:02.507
in our job hunting, 
in our relationships;

00:08:02.507 --> 00:08:04.517
when we are not feeling good about

00:08:04.517 --> 00:08:06.160
who, and what, and where we are.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.763
And I had to bring out that letter
and read it time and time again,

00:08:09.763 --> 00:08:14.135
for a period of about two weeks,
to weather me through that storm.

00:08:14.495 --> 00:08:15.778
It was important.

00:08:16.648 --> 00:08:19.789
Stop the self-talk,
the negative self-talk.

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:22.930
If you watch you'll see some athletes
that have a little bandage,

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:25.288
or a little brand around them.

00:08:25.288 --> 00:08:27.051
Lance Armstrong is a perfect one.

00:08:27.051 --> 00:08:29.289
What's his self-affirmation?

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:32.875
Livestrong isn't a brand,
it was to remind him of who he was.

00:08:32.875 --> 00:08:36.264
Live strong.
Then it became a brand.

00:08:36.264 --> 00:08:38.900
He would move that from
one arm to the next arm,

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.743
when doubt and fear
came into his mind.

00:08:41.743 --> 00:08:43.823
Live strong, put it on there, let's go.

00:08:44.685 --> 00:08:47.631
We'll all have it, we place it.

00:08:48.123 --> 00:08:49.668
Two ways to build self-confidence.

00:08:49.991 --> 00:08:50.961
I'm worried about my time

00:08:50.961 --> 00:08:52.296
I'm gonna tell you of one way

00:08:52.322 --> 00:08:54.229
you can build
self-confidence in others.

00:08:54.229 --> 00:08:56.239
We are coaches and educators,

00:08:56.239 --> 00:08:57.627
we are teachers, we are people

00:08:57.627 --> 00:08:59.339
who will create value in the world;

00:08:59.339 --> 00:09:02.992
and in doing that, we are critical
by the nature of what we do.

00:09:02.992 --> 00:09:06.015
I am a coach, 
I want you to score a goal.

00:09:06.015 --> 00:09:08.693
The ball went over high.
"Dang it!" The ball went high!

00:09:08.693 --> 00:09:12.058
"Thank you coach, I know that.
Feedback tells me that."

00:09:12.058 --> 00:09:13.013
So what do we do?

00:09:13.013 --> 00:09:15.098
I need you to put your elbow here,

00:09:15.098 --> 00:09:16.369
I need you to put
your knee over the ball,

00:09:16.369 --> 00:09:17.506
I need you to follow through.

00:09:17.506 --> 00:09:19.050
Boom. Land. Great.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:20.802
Notice, I never made it
as a professional.

00:09:20.802 --> 00:09:21.792
(Laughter)

00:09:21.792 --> 00:09:23.132
What can we do?

00:09:23.132 --> 00:09:24.774
We fix mistakes.

00:09:24.774 --> 00:09:26.211
When I'm fixing that mistake:

00:09:26.211 --> 00:09:27.282
"Johnny, this is terrible,

00:09:27.308 --> 00:09:29.631
you need to bend your knee,
you need to do this, this."

00:09:29.631 --> 00:09:32.675
What have I done to Johnny's
self-confidence?

00:09:32.675 --> 00:09:35.011
Bend your knee,
then do this, then do this.

00:09:35.011 --> 00:09:37.695
Next thing you know, 
Johnny's crushed.

00:09:38.665 --> 00:09:40.813
Ignore what Johnny does wrong

00:09:40.813 --> 00:09:43.560
and find Bob or Sally 
or Freda over here.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:47.110
Great goal Freda, I love how
you kept your knee low,

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:50.122
you followed through,
and you landed like this.

00:09:50.122 --> 00:09:51.740
Great job!

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:52.950
Johnny: "Oh?"

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:55.767
Great! Johnny's not demoralized.

00:09:55.767 --> 00:09:57.404
His confidence isn't shot,

00:09:57.404 --> 00:10:00.334
and what I've done is,
I've built up Freda's.

00:10:00.334 --> 00:10:03.903
Imagine how we could change
the way we parented kids.

00:10:03.903 --> 00:10:04.434
Instead of:

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.560
"get that glass off the counter,
what's wrong with you?"

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:07.717
(Laughter)

00:10:07.717 --> 00:10:09.888
If we catch the mother, good.

00:10:09.888 --> 00:10:12.144
Great job! Great job.

00:10:12.144 --> 00:10:14.400
Thank you Alice for taking
your glass to the counter.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.658
It sounds simple but
we forget about it.

00:10:16.658 --> 00:10:19.537
Or as educators,
or as somebody as a team,

00:10:19.537 --> 00:10:20.934
if we manage to praise

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:24.300
the positive behaviour that
we wanted to reinforce.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:26.493
We forget it.
It sounds so simple.

00:10:26.493 --> 00:10:29.139
Catch them when they're good.

00:10:29.139 --> 00:10:31.689
We forget it. It's simple.

00:10:31.689 --> 00:10:32.414
Here's what they did.

00:10:32.414 --> 00:10:33.952
There was a study
in Kansas that did this.

00:10:33.952 --> 00:10:35.744
They did video, and we all do video.

00:10:35.744 --> 00:10:38.500
And we show the video of them
doing the run of the play:

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:41.792
"Um, this goal happened because
the basket wasn't protected,

00:10:41.792 --> 00:10:43.127
we didn't rotate here, right?

00:10:43.127 --> 00:10:45.597
We needed to do this
and then cover the slot."

00:10:45.597 --> 00:10:48.030
And, if that's the baseline,

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:51.179
improvement of the Kansas
State team went like this.

00:10:51.811 --> 00:10:54.387
Then, they said they ignored all of that

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:57.539
and they just showed them
the times they did it right.

00:10:57.539 --> 00:10:59.318
The times they did it perfect.

00:10:59.318 --> 00:11:01.497
That presented no goals,
spoke to the same points,

00:11:01.497 --> 00:11:04.785
improvement went like that.

00:11:04.785 --> 00:11:07.516
It changed and revolutionized
the way we as coaches

00:11:07.516 --> 00:11:09.418
interact with our student athletes.

00:11:09.418 --> 00:11:11.612
We can apply that to the business world,

00:11:11.612 --> 00:11:13.532
we can apply that
to our student group works,

00:11:13.532 --> 00:11:15.280
we can apply that
to our management teams.

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:19.620
Easily: catch them when they are good.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.606
Last and certainly not least.

00:11:21.606 --> 00:11:24.158
My son is really good at this.

00:11:24.158 --> 00:11:26.849
Self-confident people interpret feedback

00:11:26.849 --> 00:11:29.636
the way they choose to.

00:11:29.636 --> 00:11:33.538
I ask my son who is
by the far a terrible, terrible athlete,

00:11:33.538 --> 00:11:34.462
gets it from his dad.

00:11:34.462 --> 00:11:35.796
(Laughter)

00:11:35.796 --> 00:11:36.990
The game's...

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:37.975
How's the game?

00:11:37.975 --> 00:11:41.003
Oh great! I scored three goals,
I got two assists.

00:11:41.003 --> 00:11:43.606
I'm like: "I did not see him 
touch the puck!"

00:11:43.606 --> 00:11:46.191
But he has his own perception

00:11:46.191 --> 00:11:48.010
of how he did!

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:49.267
(Laughter)

00:11:49.267 --> 00:11:50.563
I love it!

00:11:50.563 --> 00:11:51.459
(Laughter)

00:11:51.459 --> 00:11:53.727
Right? I'm the...I'm that guy!

00:11:53.727 --> 00:11:55.405
I'm like: "I remember when I was taking

00:11:55.405 --> 00:11:58.122
when I met my wife, 
it was in the commons.

00:11:58.122 --> 00:12:00.254
"Paulie, would you like 
to go to the movies?

00:12:00.254 --> 00:12:02.003
Ladies? Tingly, tingly, tingle."

00:12:02.003 --> 00:12:03.868
(Laughter)

00:12:04.798 --> 00:12:07.384
And she goes: "Ah, no."

00:12:08.804 --> 00:12:10.079
I asked her again.

00:12:10.079 --> 00:12:12.361
Cause I think that she just
hasn't seen me in the right light.

00:12:12.361 --> 00:12:13.387
(Laughter)

00:12:13.387 --> 00:12:15.096
Maybe, that's not the wrong shirt on.

00:12:15.096 --> 00:12:15.589
Right?

00:12:15.589 --> 00:12:18.603
Cause I'm interpreting that
the way I want to interpret it.

00:12:18.603 --> 00:12:20.684
Finally I asked her out again.

00:12:20.684 --> 00:12:23.732
She gave me this one comment, right?
Or, she sent it to her friend.

00:12:23.732 --> 00:12:25.015
Cause that's the way 
you did it back then.

00:12:25.015 --> 00:12:27.261
"She wouldn't date you unless

00:12:27.261 --> 00:12:28.991
there was the last person on Earth,

00:12:28.991 --> 00:12:30.314
hell was freezing over,

00:12:30.314 --> 00:12:32.990
there was a small chance
we had to save the planet Earth.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:34.689
Some people, it's like, 
there's no chance.

00:12:34.689 --> 00:12:36.676
I'm like: "You're saying
there's a chance."

00:12:36.676 --> 00:12:38.023
(Laughter)

00:12:38.023 --> 00:12:39.492
Right?

00:12:39.492 --> 00:12:42.425
Because that's how 
I'm gonna interpret it.

00:12:42.425 --> 00:12:45.610
If I could give you
one thing to take from this,

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:48.145
it is: no one will believe in you

00:12:48.145 --> 00:12:49.520
unless you do.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.312
Listen to the words of that video,

00:12:51.312 --> 00:12:53.325
here's to the crazy ones,

00:12:53.325 --> 00:12:55.645
the misfits, the rebels,

00:12:55.645 --> 00:12:57.366
the troublemakers,

00:12:57.366 --> 00:13:00.462
the round pegs in the square holes.

00:13:00.462 --> 00:13:03.028
We're supposed to be different, folks.

00:13:03.028 --> 00:13:05.269
And when people look at us,

00:13:05.269 --> 00:13:07.330
believe in yourself.

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:08.247
Thank you.

00:13:08.247 --> 00:13:12.247
(Applause)

